e/math} :answer/value "9n-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376586} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c964d1-08cb-51c1-a3d2-d470d8558b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{n}^{2}-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376587} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf572ed-6590-542e-83da-1dc4340f3685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{n}^{2}-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376588} {:answer/id #uuid "1d48e573-951e-5595-ab9b-dcd607bc7249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{n}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c964d1-08cb-51c1-a3d2-d470d8558b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{n}^{2}-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376587} :answer-field/id #uuid "811ac29a-d33c-5a01-a379-2808de32f993" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1504bd46-29b9-5e14-ada0-f70ddc5a3734" :question/math-academy-id "q-184579" :question/problem "Using the formal definition of a limit, prove that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-(-\\frac{1}{3})|<ε.\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε>0,$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-(-\\frac{1}{3})|<ε.\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $ε.$\n\nFirst, simplifying $|{a}_{n}-L|,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-(-\\frac{1}{3})| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}+\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3(1-{n}^{2})}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}+\\frac{3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3-3{n}^{2}+3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}>0$ for any natural $n\\ge 2.$ With that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n|{a}_{n}-L|=|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|=\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-(-\\frac{1}{3})|<ε,$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-(-\\frac{1}{3})| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f7e99e-94b4-5e9b-9bad-05d7b6014436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f7e99e-94b4-5e9b-9bad-05d7b6014436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377015} :answer-field/id #uuid "db8bfa7f-442b-51d5-808b-ac5c79ddf792" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c78d75d6-55e8-599e-8254-758bdadb9493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c78d75d6-55e8-599e-8254-758bdadb9493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377017} :answer-field/id #uuid "b895470a-054b-5ad4-a5ff-8d5bb48cbf4a" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377016} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af117d8d-702c-5f89-85a6-96a05f202f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af117d8d-702c-5f89-85a6-96a05f202f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377019} :answer-field/id #uuid "62ff6a4d-d728-5072-81ce-f0c70fcf798b" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4b9a14d-317b-576b-b67c-05410d05c2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b9a14d-317b-576b-b67c-05410d05c2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377021} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ca53cf-44db-561d-8ba9-ab83738f1d5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377020} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecbe735a-c5cb-5feb-b4b2-8a45d1e58c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecbe735a-c5cb-5feb-b4b2-8a45d1e58c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377023} :answer-field/id #uuid "8272e4c5-2429-505b-927b-2750e3b5d005" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1a8e997-11f9-5693-8fe0-4790fffa9c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-253879" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi ))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi ))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(\\pi ),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(\\pi )) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi )) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{1}(\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047fb130-8e41-5582-9c42-b83a6bb1146b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "047fb130-8e41-5582-9c42-b83a6bb1146b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377026} :answer-field/id #uuid "acae7b39-7953-5587-912c-6bed72a6c056" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377025} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70c17988-23a4-572d-8b74-6c4722e3c968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70c17988-23a4-572d-8b74-6c4722e3c968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377028} :answer-field/id #uuid "5705bbf5-4328-58b5-b584-6e1e7194f2ef" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377027} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0364ea93-0af0-54d2-be0c-5dfd79fa6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0364ea93-0af0-54d2-be0c-5dfd79fa6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377030} :answer-field/id #uuid "81e48ce1-6107-5825-801a-1e0617853f5e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377029} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43b8a538-70dd-5e5b-b76e-8e96ca8ca561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b8a538-70dd-5e5b-b76e-8e96ca8ca561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377032} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd78b681-ad83-5968-92bf-a31700f6b55b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a06eeb0-50da-58ee-8b6f-2f69fa647f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a06eeb0-50da-58ee-8b6f-2f69fa647f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377034} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9e2d83-4b34-5dcb-8256-50eb7278faf4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07f3343a-f24c-59e9-b03d-4ce328a45d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-253891" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $2$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =3.$ since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we have $n=3$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=3,$ we need to compute the first and second derivatives of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,⊕\\,{J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) & =({J}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\,⊕\\,({J}_{3}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d89a6c8c-1c3d-5e8e-8f2a-b1ac6ad21803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d89a6c8c-1c3d-5e8e-8f2a-b1ac6ad21803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377037} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f4974bc-e074-545a-be7a-908a290a5eed" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377036} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a12c9776-03d6-5a18-9c43-cdc37ebe7a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a12c9776-03d6-5a18-9c43-cdc37ebe7a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377039} :answer-field/id #uuid "22765ed4-0cc6-5277-848a-48261dabb73f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377038}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "192ba01b-ce48-5370-84c8-f0de9e2d73fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-274756" :question/problem "![](images/e2/e255a677948377c2811d8e0a31153084962d6d96f0f30a7df566659c6bf373d9.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the parabola shown above. Express your answer in the form $x=p{t}^{2},y=2pt,$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-2,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2,2),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2 &=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 &=2pt.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33526b37-cab0-5457-9e07-446f0c05aa08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33526b37-cab0-5457-9e07-446f0c05aa08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377042} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36531d7-495c-5197-b612-e0e22cd3b3b5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22eb775d-0ea8-57a9-a014-0d8618741de6" :question/math-academy-id "q-276278" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2t+5,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x-5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x-5}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x-5}{2}-2)(\\frac{x-5}{2}+1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x-5-4}{2})(\\frac{x-5+2}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x-9}{2})(\\frac{x-3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x-9)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8e2d09a-689d-5e58-a00e-c11460b13e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e2d09a-689d-5e58-a00e-c11460b13e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377045} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad9a621-dd17-56d4-9033-7136556fc8ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377044} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "843a74a1-39ae-5459-aac3-eba8e54c75d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "843a74a1-39ae-5459-aac3-eba8e54c75d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377047} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0622f5b-ad86-55ca-876c-6179afbb1ce5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "659b155d-7a86-5ce4-a60f-a888671f046c" :question/math-academy-id "q-280198" :question/problem "![](images/23/23fa9b0f268e953cf9a2b770c3a3cb83c64fa0217a55e3a5313c81bf6933fc0a.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(1,2).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(1,2),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=2$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=2pt \\\\ 2 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2pt \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{2p})}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{4{p}^{2}} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{4p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{1}{8})t,\\,y=(\\frac{1}{8}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{4}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37408f16-b913-5fff-9939-14af701125d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37408f16-b913-5fff-9939-14af701125d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377050} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a105c3-b080-5c79-b83a-4614140f393e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f907296-0315-56bf-93ac-6dd5b6b8873c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f907296-0315-56bf-93ac-6dd5b6b8873c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377052} :answer-field/id #uuid "0129630e-e038-5d41-a51e-42a6c95e0206" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b48ff2f6-1f4f-57fa-a4be-c6b32ca75df3" :question/math-academy-id "q-280217" :question/problem "![](images/11/115dcd6d4fe22466bc563f93f278c2fca6af4274b731efeb781504b5d41d7490.png)\n\nDetermine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate the above parabola. Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(4,-1),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-1$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=2pt \\\\ -1 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -1 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(-4)t,\\,y=(-4){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-8t,\\,y=-4{t}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf01222a-068e-5d54-8d03-fce66b9c0ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf01222a-068e-5d54-8d03-fce66b9c0ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377055} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba131a57-7ffc-54c1-b039-86518a03e7c3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "381c11eb-ff0a-5749-8cc6-3754e25db56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "381c11eb-ff0a-5749-8cc6-3754e25db56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377057} :answer-field/id #uuid "853d807b-7dba-5d70-bfb7-fe5e4f29330d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b87ecaed-5da1-505a-bfd4-108852c3407b" :question/math-academy-id "q-280221" :question/problem "Determine a set of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ that generate a vertical parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-2,4).$ Express your answer in the form $x=2pt,y=p{t}^{2},$ where $p$ is a constant.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-2,4),$ we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2 &=2pt \\\\ 4 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =2pt \\\\ t & =-\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(-\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(\\frac{1}{4})t,\\,y=(\\frac{1}{4}){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377067 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dea13ba0-1a30-5294-9ce2-1654ed4bab1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40+\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dea13ba0-1a30-5294-9ce2-1654ed4bab1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40+\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377069} :answer-field/id #uuid "c60e8ff8-8e89-5160-a0d8-53fd5813b77e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d1669262-044b-56c9-938a-8d7361fae453" :question/math-academy-id "q-316251" :question/problem "Water is being poured into a storage tank at a rate of $5t$ liters per minute. If at time $t=0$ the tank already contains $40$ liters of water, write an expression for $V(t),$ the total volume of water in the tank at time $t,$ where $t$ is measured in minutes.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(t)=5t$ represent the rate at which water enters the tank, and let $V(T)$ be the total volume of water in the tank after $T$ minutes.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nV(T)=V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(T) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{T}5t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={40+(\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =40+\\frac{5{T}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nV(t)=40+\\frac{5{t}^{2}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377082 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "444c47c5-f6ff-5993-9810-f2a59d46e7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444c47c5-f6ff-5993-9810-f2a59d46e7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377084} :answer-field/id #uuid "505822d3-763e-5bdd-b961-64b75dcd3eb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377083}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c9558ce-37ee-554b-ae06-97c014ed01c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319828" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n{{field-1}}$=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2}+{(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e76ec15d-4235-56b9-b033-fb60f2aa5631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e76ec15d-4235-56b9-b033-fb60f2aa5631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e1d208-e4d2-5915-83c1-b4b15806c88a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "081bb8fa-7465-5289-83b2-398e18ea3051" :question/math-academy-id "q-320878" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3t-1,\\,\\,\\,y=(t+5)(t-2),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3t-1\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{x+1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{x+1}{3}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(\\frac{x+1}{3}+5)(\\frac{x+1}{3}-2) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x+1+15}{3})(\\frac{x+1-6}{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{x+16}{3})(\\frac{x-5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}(x+16)(x-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfd39e46-6e10-5277-9820-57dd084b55b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd39e46-6e10-5277-9820-57dd084b55b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377090} :answer-field/id #uuid "3733f8ba-81a2-59af-8065-3da7adf791ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0deecc2-15f7-5c26-991c-e45282132b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-321315" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this curve is\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4} \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the double-angle formula for cosine and the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\frac{x}{4}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t=\\frac{y}{3}$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3} & =2{(-\\frac{x}{4})}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-1 \\\\ y & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{8}-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da930ca-7fbc-5c42-9912-9b1b52ebdd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377134}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da930ca-7fbc-5c42-9912-9b1b52ebdd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377134} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a10ec52-7232-5c11-84d5-518e58b72bfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377133} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8a46cd-d5a5-5ccc-8455-5de699490c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd8a46cd-d5a5-5ccc-8455-5de699490c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377136} :answer-field/id #uuid "e691efc5-3efd-5054-ac9a-ea55ec9a29ac" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d71c568-9579-5f11-98da-9ed03aa688e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-332888" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-14y=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n7\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-14{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n7(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-14(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(14\\beta -14\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-14\\alpha -14\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}14\\beta -14\\alpha &=-8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -14\\alpha -14\\beta &=3\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{5}{28},\\beta =-\\frac{11}{28}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{5}{28}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{11}{28}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{5}{28})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{11}{28})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66750a16-700f-5c23-a55a-30496608f331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377200} {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} {:answer/id #uuid "0d67e761-f48e-5810-bebf-b6e7784e791c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377202} {:answer/id #uuid "0facc8a8-1164-510f-a7f4-a0e94be929e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377203} {:answer/id #uuid "77e0c788-e151-5687-a6d5-ff813c78241e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67dc3a2a-113e-5c48-a4d5-b5f7e81c6779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377201} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc7f9793-5f2d-5fa5-9828-29145c21e291" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2fb266c-0e36-5075-b402-7e3b0f1013d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336506" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir in a manufacturing plant can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of coolant solution with $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor. A more concentrated coolant solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $21\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $70\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $21\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $14\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=70+21t-14t=7(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $14\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(14\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(14\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14Q}{7(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10.5-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 10.5 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =10.5(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =10.5(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 10.5(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =10.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ 3.5(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ 35+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =20 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-15 \\\\ C & =-15(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.5(10+t)-\\frac{15(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.5(10+t)-15{(\\frac{10}{10+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 70+21{t}_{o}-14{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 70+7{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 7{t}_{o} & =140 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3.5(10+20)-15{(\\frac{10}{10+20})}^{2} \\\\  & =105-15{(\\frac{10}{30})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈103.3\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d60df00e-8828-5ca8-b2ed-d8bd599eb07f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377207} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa07fe2-d9eb-58e7-adb1-02efd7fc679e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377208} {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} {:answer/id #uuid "1dbf4199-9fdf-5b11-9f3c-4a9a9611b20f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377210} {:answer/id #uuid "e6800f5a-8ca4-5d4d-a314-681c9b7bdc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "94.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8206d7a-b002-5297-86c6-ed2e4ff17c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377209} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a930be-e052-5424-bf3a-7194b15d9c7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47a16983-dbf1-596b-b57d-9f38439fdd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-336513" :question/problem "A flavoring tank at a beverage factory can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of flavoring solution with $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved flavoring. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which flavoring enters the tank minus the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of flavoring enters the tank at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *flavoring* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =6\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which flavoring exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which flavoring leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $75\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=75+9t-6t=3(25+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of flavoring in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *flavoring* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(25+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{2Q}{25+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q=6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(25+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|25+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(25+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(25+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{25+t}\\,Q) & =(25+t)^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\ (25+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(25+t)Q & =6(25+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2}) & =6(25+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(25+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 6(25+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(25+t)^{2} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(25+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2(25+t)+\\frac{C}{(25+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $25\\,\\text{lb}$ of flavoring. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=25,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =25 \\\\ 2(25+0)+\\frac{C}{(25+0)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ 50+\\frac{C}{(25)^{2}} & =25 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{25}^{2}} & =-25 \\\\ C & =-25(25)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2(25+t)-\\frac{25(25)^{2}}{(25+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2(25+t)-25{(\\frac{25}{25+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 75+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 75+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =75 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of flavoring in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2(25+25)-25{(\\frac{25}{25+25})}^{2} \\\\  & =100-25{(\\frac{25}{50})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈93.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e3931a-6dfb-5fbc-8b0f-dbd997b8f656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377214} {:answer/id #uuid "0562dc6c-24dc-5a4f-a3f1-00a900d6d777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377215} {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} {:answer/id #uuid "976b22bc-fda8-59e2-abbb-17c2352700de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "157.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377217} {:answer/id #uuid "a8deab91-7c4b-52d1-bbed-ff4091b43b8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "159.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46790c34-6fdb-5081-bdf0-ae69ba6762d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "158.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377216} :answer-field/id #uuid "5df70905-5278-5516-abae-b8ae4602e0e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "729a2fd5-523f-5eb2-bf2f-20fedeba14f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336516" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $200\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $50\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $10\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=50+15t-10t=5(10+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{5(10+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{10+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(10+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|10+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(10+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{10+t}\\,Q) & =(10+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (10+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(10+t)Q & =12(10+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2}) & =12(10+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(10+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(10+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(10+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(10+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(10+t)+\\frac{C}{(10+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 4(10+0)+\\frac{C}{(10+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 40+\\frac{C}{(10)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{10}^{2}} & =-30 \\\\ C & =-30(10)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(10+t)-\\frac{30(10)^{2}}{(10+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(10+t)-30{(\\frac{10}{10+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $200\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=200$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =200 \\\\ 50+15{t}_{o}-10{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 50+5{t}_{o} & =200 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(10+30)-30{(\\frac{10}{10+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =160-30{(\\frac{10}{40})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈158.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c4ccf1-eb7e-5b5f-a3cf-60eae080271f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3y+4z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377237} {:answer/id #uuid "ae591169-a058-56e3-b4b4-ac2b45753920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y-3z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377238} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f71240-ae22-528e-99ab-71f715fa697c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377239} {:answer/id #uuid "abd101c7-b915-5fd8-9dfe-6a323905a8ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4y-3z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377240} {:answer/id #uuid "66040242-4691-579e-beba-f5503538cc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+3z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae591169-a058-56e3-b4b4-ac2b45753920" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y-3z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377238} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dc189f4-275b-5dfe-b268-f73759792b0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bedcf41a-1f98-5cfb-aa75-b3495ee5c2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-33667" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨-1,0,0⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2},4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩=⟨0,3,4⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-1,0,0⟩\\times (-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,3,4⟩) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are interested only in the direction of the above vector, let's write our perpendicular vector $n$ as \n\n$$\nn=(-5)\\cdot (T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=⟨0,4,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ denote the position vector of a point on the osculating plane. Since the plane is perpendicular to $n$ and passes through the tip of the position vector $p$, its Cartesian equation can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(⟨x,y,z⟩-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,-3⟩ & =⟨0,3,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,4,-3⟩ \\\\ 0\\cdot x+4\\cdot y+(-3)\\cdot z & =0 \\\\ 4y-3z & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a3f26d4-7fd0-5dfc-b30a-66eaf31e8b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.34\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377251} {:answer/id #uuid "59e1559d-1a25-5f5b-86a0-b44ad480a144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17.94\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377252} {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} {:answer/id #uuid "3dca446e-f8bb-5a17-9561-bc2230c8306c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.42\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377254} {:answer/id #uuid "9010ad44-b315-5215-8406-035745dd6ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.01\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6884f190-c170-5c58-85a7-6a4669b950fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.04\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377253} :answer-field/id #uuid "753683c1-dfc0-5785-8a4b-d335ca58b088" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01365019-dd55-56aa-aa06-e538a69722fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-336743" :question/problem "A processing tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved preservative.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative is pumped into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of preservative in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which preservative enters the tank minus the rate at which preservative leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which preservative enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the preservative enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *preservative* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which preservative exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which preservative leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $18\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+18t-12t=(420+6t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of preservative in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *preservative* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420+6t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{2Q}{70+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q=0.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(70+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|70+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(70+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(70+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(70+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{70+t}Q) & =(70+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.9 \\\\ (70+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(70+t)Q & =0.9(70+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2}) & =0.9(70+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(70+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.9(70+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(70+t)^{2} & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(70+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)+\\frac{C}{(70+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of preservative. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 0.3(70+0)+\\frac{C}{(70+0)^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 21+\\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{70}^{2}} & =-11 \\\\ C & =-11(70)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(70+t)-\\frac{11(70)^{2}}{(70+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.3(70+t)-11{(\\frac{70}{70+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of preservative in the tank $15$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =0.3(70+15)-11{(\\frac{70}{70+15})}^{2} \\\\  & =25.5-11{(\\frac{70}{85})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈18.04\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377270 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e02ad9-f01e-5d91-b67e-c8ac9b2f9690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32e02ad9-f01e-5d91-b67e-c8ac9b2f9690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377272} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7d1f7d0-ad3b-599f-aa11-6740016d6811" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcce442e-f4af-544c-929a-c36fe279cfc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-336815" :question/problem "![](images/97/97d661baf511c1e99ee8eed7fbed2ad02d9f96c452ece5b765a3e2cf657c431b.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=1\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=6I+\\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta &=10\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{3},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}≈1.49\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56be3e3d-cc68-5b73-870d-17bf13a0d689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56be3e3d-cc68-5b73-870d-17bf13a0d689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377275} :answer-field/id #uuid "207cd688-1b68-5ccb-98fe-558f7b0217d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c771bb1c-fa6d-50e6-96f6-b48fda5177fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-336817" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b7ddd9e65fdbd1c8756c4cbf5690640736fb91c5d93bdba941ba62decb0bbf4.png)\n\nFind the amplitude of the steady-state current for the RL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\nThe amplitude of the steady-state current is approximately\n{{field-1}} amperes." :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $4\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=4\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=4I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=4I+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+8I=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+8{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))+8(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+(8\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +8\\beta &=8\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\ 8\\alpha +2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{17},$ $\\beta =\\frac{16}{17}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-8t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{4}{17}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{16}{17}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(-\\frac{4}{17})}^{2}+{(\\frac{16}{17})}^{2}}≈0.97\\,\\text{A},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377293 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} {:answer/id #uuid "d27a819d-3733-5724-9e0a-425c1701164a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377296} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac1a8be-c213-5433-be78-80bb938c8e36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377297} {:answer/id #uuid "dba1b189-1202-5081-99fc-577118ac8811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377298} {:answer/id #uuid "b25a95ae-28ad-5afb-9758-ae8c6c582bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80c9af41-db22-5fdd-9f0e-114cc03d4dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377295} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c0a0d5-4bf3-54b2-a43d-067aa74d6301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "456aacfd-49e4-5a1e-8363-4f5725f307ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-339394" :question/problem "A juice-processing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sugar $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a liquid solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $20\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+20t-30t=(400-10t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{400-10t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{3Q}{40-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(40-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|40-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(40-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(40-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(40-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{40-t}Q) & =(40-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (40-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(40-t)^{-4}Q & =0.6(40-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3}) & =0.6(40-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(40-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(40-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(40-t)^{-3} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(40-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)+C(40-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ C(40-0)^{3}+0.3(40-0) & =4 \\\\ C(40)^{3}+12 & =4 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.3(40-t)-\\frac{8}{{40}^{3}}(40-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 40\\,(\\frac{40-t}{40})-8{(\\frac{40-t}{40})}^{3} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{40})-8{(1-\\frac{t}{40})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-8,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-8)=20.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377300 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f681e08e-700d-5f11-a546-1b73d4a5d646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377302} {:answer/id #uuid "b28e0e34-3fc5-53eb-9f95-3e5de6d384b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377303} {:answer/id #uuid "4b12db81-cdb5-5c78-aff0-867122be9ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377304} {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} {:answer/id #uuid "87a222e1-6c03-558b-9d4f-e7aa60b98bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efe5c524-df1e-58a3-a335-25cf075363db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377305} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c722bf6-15d0-5bfc-a832-c8c844cbaa11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "35a9dbe2-5b3d-52d0-b235-b8068a9e0f15" :question/math-academy-id "q-339396" :question/problem "A fruit syrup tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved sweetener.\nA solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved sweetener is pumped into the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of sweetener $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sweetener enters the tank minus the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener enter the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of sweetener enters the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sweetener* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sweetener exit the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sweetener leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $24\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $32\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+24t-32t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sweetener in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $32\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sweetener* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(32\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(32\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{4Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q=1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-4}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-4}\\cdot 1.2 \\\\ (30-t)^{-4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(30-t)^{-5}Q & =1.2(30-t)^{-4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4}) & =1.2(30-t)^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-4})\\,dt & =\\int 1.2(30-t)^{-4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-4} & =1.2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30-t)^{-3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)+C(30-t)^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $7\\,\\text{kg}$ of sweetener. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=7,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =7 \\\\ C(30-0)^{4}+0.4(30-0) & =7 \\\\ C(30)^{4}+12 & =7 \\\\ C & =-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.4(30-t)-\\frac{5}{{30}^{4}}(30-t)^{4} \\\\  & =0.4\\cdot 30\\,(\\frac{30-t}{30})-5{(\\frac{30-t}{30})}^{4} \\\\  & =12\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})-5{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =12,\\beta =-5,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =12-(-5)=17.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377307 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7203da68-a7dc-5fa7-a821-14e51c37d796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.21\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377309} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c3b57f-b0fe-5ca4-bc6e-be09e8b780b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.97\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377311} {:answer/id #uuid "57a068be-87ec-53cf-a457-ecd6c727cbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.92\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377312} {:answer/id #uuid "08fda585-15a3-51ba-b2f7-6c5b1fb1b63f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10.88\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377313}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6e3ac6-f774-5924-a271-5096cb694bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.44\\,\\text{kg}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377310} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa11ba31-9d56-5bad-87cb-702df2d10932" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6bee6b8-8870-5e26-916b-bf3b0601ff8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-339399" :question/problem "A coolant reservoir initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved corrosion inhibitor.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor is pumped into the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins." :question/worked-solution "Let $Q$ be the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which inhibitor enters the tank minus the rate at which inhibitor leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor enters the reservoir.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the inhibitor enters the reservoir at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *inhibitor* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which inhibitor exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which inhibitor leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $12\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n400+16t-12t=(400+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{L/min},$ *inhibitor* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{400+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{3Q}{100+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q=0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(100+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|100+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(100+t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(100+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(100+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{100+t}Q) & =(100+t)^{3}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\ (100+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(100+t)^{2}Q & =0.8(100+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3}) & =0.8(100+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(100+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.8(100+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(100+t)^{3} & =0.8\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(100+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)+\\frac{C}{(100+t)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of inhibitor. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ 0.2(100+0)+\\frac{C}{(100+0)^{3}} & =6 \\\\ 20+\\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{100}^{3}} & =-14 \\\\ C & =-14(100)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(100+t)-\\frac{14(100)^{3}}{(100+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =0.2(100+t)-14{(\\frac{100}{100+t})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of inhibitor in the reservoir $12$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =0.2(100+12)-14{(\\frac{100}{100+12})}^{3} \\\\  & =22.4-14{(\\frac{25}{28})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈12.44\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377333 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71611be5-2f86-54a6-8fa3-7e638fa517d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71611be5-2f86-54a6-8fa3-7e638fa517d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377335} :answer-field/id #uuid "00ddeb05-c0e1-5f48-974e-94b884900045" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b50bf9af-d03d-53c0-93cf-2aa4ad5a2234" :question/math-academy-id "q-340473" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{12}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377336 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afe8e06b-8a1b-5efd-8e1b-855c951f5a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afe8e06b-8a1b-5efd-8e1b-855c951f5a55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377338} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd5b9f84-42a3-57ce-9ab1-dbc551ef01e8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc5d8ff1-9935-5447-83ed-23682e5487b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340476" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(0-1)-\\frac{1}{16}(0-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf3cf48-269f-57ac-b6b0-19f358507270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf3cf48-269f-57ac-b6b0-19f358507270" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377341} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ec02afa-ff5c-5a8a-83f9-f12941e64e28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "966c3f67-ba27-5242-834e-e749248a884e" :question/math-academy-id "q-340488" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(-1-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11b8e7d7-477f-5630-a348-70b06bf429f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11b8e7d7-477f-5630-a348-70b06bf429f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377344} :answer-field/id #uuid "566d1b4e-0853-51e9-a9d2-e9886b9f1fee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8899fff-852c-5526-b193-628c43b1b225" :question/math-academy-id "q-340493" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x+5x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x-5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)]}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}(1-1)-\\frac{1}{12}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377357 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc1ff452-6201-5bba-925d-201ebec3889a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc1ff452-6201-5bba-925d-201ebec3889a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377359} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a213c59-8600-547a-887b-f9a2e5ec68ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6c68127-8372-5d4a-9ede-b7594ed5b877" :question/math-academy-id "q-342014" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<2, \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}x+4, & x\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{5}(-\\frac{1}{2}x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}){|}_{-1}^{2}\\,+\\,(-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+4x){|}_{2}^{5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3}-(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)^{3})]\\,+\\,[-\\frac{1}{4}(5)^{2}+4(5)-(-\\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2}+4(2))] \\\\  & =[\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})]\\,+\\,[-\\frac{25}{4}+20-(-1+8)] \\\\  & =3+\\frac{27}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377411 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7743b496-84dc-50c9-94d0-2d737728d0fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}t+2\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7743b496-84dc-50c9-94d0-2d737728d0fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}t+2\\operatorname{sin}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377413} :answer-field/id #uuid "6caaf403-1251-5c5f-b630-d77aa497a6fc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af9ce2b8-8fda-5adb-a5c8-8596858d3333" :question/math-academy-id "q-345086" :question/problem "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-y(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)-3s-2-Y(s)=-4(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1})-6(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)-3s-2-Y(s) & =-4(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1})-6(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =3s+2-\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+1}-\\frac{6s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =\\frac{(3s+2)({s}^{2}-1)}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3s+2}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>1.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{3s}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{2}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =3{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}}+2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\  \\\\ y(t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93d8d729-4ef7-503e-99f9-b3f05f898442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}t-2\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93d8d729-4ef7-503e-99f9-b3f05f898442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}t-2\\operatorname{cos}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377416} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32f2d92-9646-5cbc-ade1-ea9a9c512ad1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "596c294a-df7d-56e3-8cd3-e56205512a55" :question/math-academy-id "q-345087" :question/problem "Find $y(t)$ given that the Laplace transform of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(t)-y(t)=-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n{s}^{2}Y(s)+2s-5-Y(s)=-10(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1})+4(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nThe solution is $y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the transformed equation, we can solve for $Y(s)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}Y(s)+2s-5-Y(s) & =-10(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1})+4(\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =5-2s-\\frac{10}{{s}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-1)Y(s) & =\\frac{(-2s+5)({s}^{2}-1)}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{-2s+5}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1}-\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is valid for $s>1.$\n\nNow, taking the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}(Y(s)) & ={L}^{-1}{\\frac{5}{{s}^{2}+1}-\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\  & =5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+1}}-2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}} \\\\ y(t) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377523 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4c492a7-c6cb-572c-b421-279ac6ba0b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4c492a7-c6cb-572c-b421-279ac6ba0b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377525} :answer-field/id #uuid "82146a81-10d4-5c1a-b990-221c17b83407" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377524}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee7106c8-947f-5aeb-a162-7183b03b32c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-346536" :question/problem "Calculate $F(2)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n$F(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(2)={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16(-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{-2})\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16(-\\frac{1}{8}-(-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =16(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =16(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f893fb43-07ca-5e57-9357-08e378e4d081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f893fb43-07ca-5e57-9357-08e378e4d081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377528} :answer-field/id #uuid "67caecad-ce6e-5278-84f7-34f37ebabbc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377527}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26e986f8-6233-563d-8d4c-878685495738" :question/math-academy-id "q-346556" :question/problem "Calculate $F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{x}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dadac5c6-a33d-5ec0-8085-af08c5a4bf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dadac5c6-a33d-5ec0-8085-af08c5a4bf3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377534} :answer-field/id #uuid "84701d8c-3c1c-5940-b9a1-9b8b85613cc8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "83c90feb-6b72-56fb-a3aa-8a0423cce487" :question/math-academy-id "q-346578" :question/problem "The total charge, in coulombs, stored in a device $t$ seconds after charging begins increases at a rate of $\\frac{40}{(t+1)^{3}}.$ If, at $t=0$ seconds, the device already stored $7$ coulombs of charge, how much charge will the device store after $1$ second?\n\nAfter $1$ second, the device stores\n{{field-1}} coulombs of charge." :question/worked-solution "Let $a(t)=\\frac{40}{(t+1)^{3}}$ be the rate at which the amount of charge stored is changing and let $A(T)$ be the amount of charge stored $T$ seconds after charging begins. \n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nA(T)=A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(T) & =A(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7+{\\int }_{0}^{T}\\frac{40}{(t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =7+(-\\frac{20}{(t+1)^{2}}){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =7+(-\\frac{20}{(T+1)^{2}})-(-\\frac{20}{{1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =7-\\frac{20}{(T+1)^{2}}+20 \\\\  & =27-\\frac{20}{(T+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the amount of charge stored $T=1$ second after charging begins, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nA(1)=7-\\frac{20}{(1+1)^{2}}+20=22.\n$$\n\nThus, after $1$ second, the device stores $22$ coulombs of charge."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377538 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d90c6aca-5f52-581d-b9b7-53525c39db0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377540} {:answer/id #uuid "35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35bc1324-3515-5f3b-b9da-7c903cdfb324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427994} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d0f7cff-e86b-56b3-9a00-cbb02d98343e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4068a5d2-2d7d-5115-9e90-ed45feea5f4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-351845" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{10}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{10}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{10}{9\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{10}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{10}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)-\\frac{10}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377565 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0d41d5-97e5-5322-a0ae-be7ae39fb408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e0d41d5-97e5-5322-a0ae-be7ae39fb408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377567} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1956f37-de74-5c76-8118-3d54690a9b34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d847b8f0-2f89-5f9a-b9ae-502b6d7ea9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-352044" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 12{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}(4{x}^{3})-\\int (4{x}^{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{9}-8D \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{8}{9})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{8}{9})]}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =[{(\\frac{e}{2})}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{e}{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{e}{2})+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & \\,-[{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{8}{9})]-[\\frac{1}{8}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}(4-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{9})]-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377594 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "595c803c-cc37-5acf-8d28-de44643dd96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377596} {:answer/id #uuid "6b35406a-3f18-5606-a66f-0a7b876dc018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377597} {:answer/id #uuid "7a819d87-f9d2-5963-8a20-1374245554cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377598} {:answer/id #uuid "a787adc8-db92-5c25-b5df-9bd12f891aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377599} {:answer/id #uuid "32684054-2142-5926-928b-7bee6a4c48e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377600}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "595c803c-cc37-5acf-8d28-de44643dd96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377596} :answer-field/id #uuid "f324bdac-71ef-525e-9a4a-7e229f1a660d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377595}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "028b6dfa-ac89-5974-9044-6dccd3022ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49812" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-x+D) \\\\  & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={(x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2x)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(e{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+2e)-(1{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+2(1)) \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14964e6e-52eb-5450-b834-6f39c564cfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377603} {:answer/id #uuid "68eef503-e36d-5b2b-bd29-d67addd4c6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377604} {:answer/id #uuid "69916269-fb09-53fd-a347-c2e68e4539fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377605} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbbfec6-481a-5e83-ba2f-adebd25d605b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377606} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1561d2-618c-5f0b-b12f-888d32558663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68eef503-e36d-5b2b-bd29-d67addd4c6bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377604} :answer-field/id #uuid "3beb6bc3-f053-5e21-b0a5-364dd2833706" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "49213088-f7e8-5812-af4e-2e9471833802" :question/math-academy-id "q-51224" :question/problem "Calculate $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $F(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nF\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nThen, computing the definite integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bf0d995-cf84-56bf-afd8-392394482e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3(s+3)}{({s}^{2}-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377623} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb312fa-6976-5a16-b895-e40bbbb146aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{6}{(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377624} {:answer/id #uuid "1ce4e2fb-c06d-500a-9610-2e6e2b691e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{s+1}{(s-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377625} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b7da03-3ee1-5046-b7fa-7cd6e10fc320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+3s}{(s-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377626} {:answer/id #uuid "2b755909-4646-56f4-bdbf-32f65222e59a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{{s}^{2}+6}{({s}^{2}-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bf0d995-cf84-56bf-afd8-392394482e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y(s)=\\frac{3(s+3)}{({s}^{2}-1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377623} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9e03a0f-26f0-5eb4-9cf1-aecfcd606036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e638c897-cf8d-51b8-bf0c-3808e3a5ccc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-64364" :question/problem "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=6{e}^{-t},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y} & =L{6{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}-3L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =6L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L{y}=Y(s).$\n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{′}}$ and $L{{y}^{″}},$  we use formulas for the Laplace transforms of the derivatives and apply the initial conditions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{′}} & =sY(s)-y(0)=sY(s) \\\\ L{{y}^{″}} & ={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}-3L{{y}^{′}}+2L{y} & =6L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-3)-3(sY(s))+2Y(s) & =6(\\frac{1}{s+1}) \\\\ ({s}^{2}-3s+2)Y(s) & =3+\\frac{6}{s+1} \\\\ (s-1)(s-2)Y(s) & =\\frac{3s+9}{s+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3(s+3)}{(s+1)(s-1)(s-2)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{3(s+3)}{({s}^{2}-1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377777 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c819a8-69c1-5f0d-ba40-eb78fd2be723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377779} {:answer/id #uuid "39f59b00-cc03-55ab-b6b8-1ab9c1ffeae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377780} {:answer/id #uuid "b4a6473f-4085-5582-85ae-4516ab8e6591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377781} {:answer/id #uuid "f7652fff-4d92-5f23-894d-77bffbcfe791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377782} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa70a7e-2036-5502-bde5-0309ee363352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7652fff-4d92-5f23-894d-77bffbcfe791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377782} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0a1617d-d91d-575d-bb6f-36a897794ae3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "729c5739-583c-5a71-86c4-2b95ece46c63" :question/math-academy-id "q-80615" :question/problem "Find the curvature at an arbitrary point on the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by its arc length $s$, where:\n\n$$\nr(s)=⟨\\frac{3s+1}{5},\\,\\frac{3-4s}{5}⟩,\\,s\\ge 0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $T(s)$ denote the unit tangent to the given curve as a function of the arc length $s.$ Since we are given the curve $r(s)$ parametrized by the arc length $s$, we will use the following formula for the curvature:\n\n$$\nκ(s)=‖{T}^{′}(s)‖=‖{r}^{″}(s)‖\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(s)$ and ${r}^{″}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3s+1}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3-4s}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{5},\\,-\\frac{4}{5}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(s) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{3}{5}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{4}{5})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(s) & =‖{r}^{″}(s)‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec9f779c-6b56-5a47-b5ce-8ecc7e15bfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=24{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377902} {:answer/id #uuid "faf2ab90-1944-5dbc-a1cb-77420b1da390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377903} {:answer/id #uuid "24c1fb89-78ee-5403-94f4-14694d89539b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377904} {:answer/id #uuid "ceda9423-24e0-57eb-b69d-8e72177caace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377905} {:answer/id #uuid "143d7c5c-3a49-594c-b23d-a77f8e9fe684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2t,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377906}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf2ab90-1944-5dbc-a1cb-77420b1da390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=6t,\\,\\,y=3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377903} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3ebdc11-f05d-553b-a7a6-a6b674bb37b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8bf28229-05a6-5b2e-8652-9947e15e4e53" :question/math-academy-id "q-84672" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d67b2209b622bd26ce7e4ee488ca02208bb120fcd0ab01c602db67da1291dc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is vertical, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(6,3).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a vertical parabola  that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=2pt,\\,y=p{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(6,3),$ we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=3$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}6 &=2pt \\\\ 3 &=p{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =2pt \\\\ 3 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =p{(\\frac{3}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 3 & =p\\cdot \\frac{9}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{9}{p} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=2(3)t,\\,y=(3){t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=6t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377907 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4d9489e-dd11-577b-96d4-d5035924910d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377909} {:answer/id #uuid "64de172d-06fe-5b36-9a15-78bdbab10f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377910} {:answer/id #uuid "5ceb9fe9-4c19-551d-aa2f-64372fe2f34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377911} {:answer/id #uuid "eec93caa-7a54-5a50-90c2-fab1b8919ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377912} {:answer/id #uuid "83673830-6982-5f5a-9fbf-fe32a83d8688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec93caa-7a54-5a50-90c2-fab1b8919ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377912} :answer-field/id #uuid "11366e17-f910-51f5-9dfe-4bbb278b8729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "774f4745-f83d-5677-acf1-c9ba670abf97" :question/math-academy-id "q-84712" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-2x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-2x \\\\ 4p & =-2 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a1f6e3e-fc44-588b-9361-870043651a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377916} {:answer/id #uuid "0bfb2484-2d7b-5992-87c9-6572c701b31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377917} {:answer/id #uuid "46e8cd84-209a-502d-bd2a-77caf0a01df7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377918} {:answer/id #uuid "956d63af-5290-5e47-8cca-0835e08568c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377919} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd10e2d-47a2-5fef-86a6-42cd963e8cea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-\\frac{3}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a1f6e3e-fc44-588b-9361-870043651a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y=-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377916} :answer-field/id #uuid "a54c1b7a-0493-5402-98f0-4f45b80d8a96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a70332e-8a80-5ac7-8e93-195113807a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-84715" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation ${y}^{2}=-6x?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola ${y}^{2}=4px$ that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case ${y}^{2}=4px$ and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4px & =-6x \\\\ 4p & =-6 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-\\frac{3}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-\\frac{3}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y=-3t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cfad461-790b-5508-af1b-6805aadb0289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377964} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fc8ada-4f57-5d87-b517-21a3ca68e2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-16,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377965} {:answer/id #uuid "c494714f-7193-5ec7-876c-fe6709380caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377966} {:answer/id #uuid "62e00f58-5de6-5179-8ad6-0280cff2533e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377967} {:answer/id #uuid "96b22878-a540-53b9-9f81-9ee8775c3d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e00f58-5de6-5179-8ad6-0280cff2533e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377967} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda3649a-ac4e-51ba-8f62-cf9e13c454ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a9071e54-31a2-59dd-bc25-80b2c2dae136" :question/math-academy-id "q-85064" :question/problem "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y+6)^{2}=-6(x+8)?$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y+6)^{2}=4(-\\frac{6}{4})(x+8),\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n(y+6)^{2}=4(-\\frac{3}{2})(x+8).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-8$ and $k=-6.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-8,-6).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378111 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7918c708-8a12-53ff-9189-39fafe93a6ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{t}^{2}+3}{{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378113} {:answer/id #uuid "5c91dcfb-6f4a-5a2f-828d-4058552877aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378114} {:answer/id #uuid "c142af18-99c4-5932-a243-68d3ee5ec0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{t+2}{{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378115} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf0b0d1-8add-5231-a63e-e6ab0414f4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378116} {:answer/id #uuid "bf09cf53-fe49-50e0-87d6-8b054d366ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf0b0d1-8add-5231-a63e-e6ab0414f4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378116} :answer-field/id #uuid "2930f83f-6300-522b-ad6a-8570be1d88e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "787a6f42-c967-5cc6-9076-7ab2d3575e31" :question/math-academy-id "q-97604" :question/problem "The position $r$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,\\frac{1}{t},\\,-1⟩.$ Find the tangential component of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The tangential component of acceleration is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{T}=\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the velocity vector by differentiating the position vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the acceleration vector by differentiating the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $v\\cdot a$ and $||v||:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot a & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},\\,\\frac{2}{{t}^{3}},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{3}}-\\frac{2}{{t}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{5}} \\\\ ||v|| & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{t})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the tangential component of the acceleration, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{T} & =\\frac{v\\cdot a}{||v||} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{5}})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}{{t}^{2}})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f3bea2c-306c-5e48-88e0-f498569d09ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378424} {:answer/id #uuid "10399da4-e690-5c4d-b2a4-9bff2c1171d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378425} {:answer/id #uuid "a1078051-d4e1-5037-957d-9cb4bee113ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378426} {:answer/id #uuid "0308c1f2-6766-5c2d-93c2-954e116c5e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378427} {:answer/id #uuid "c831d6cb-31f4-5404-8eed-6b17d2e24673" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10399da4-e690-5c4d-b2a4-9bff2c1171d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378425} :answer-field/id #uuid "219e25d5-1a5d-54bd-84e6-7016995fdf31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4507ae6a-461d-5502-b3e2-7bf57bad8732" :question/math-academy-id "q-113180" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is $x.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the term $\\frac{2}{x}$ becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35af3004-f259-5b19-aa35-79f2c60b07fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378707} {:answer/id #uuid "8cd51b97-b681-5911-aaa5-a0a6ad7c2ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378708} {:answer/id #uuid "c397ae1a-b819-5611-b646-82b07c59a31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378709} {:answer/id #uuid "5bed2431-2fed-56cd-806b-95d255fe8f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378710} {:answer/id #uuid "149dcb06-274a-5248-89a5-7188c42b03ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bed2431-2fed-56cd-806b-95d255fe8f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378710} :answer-field/id #uuid "9843f025-965b-5496-8a1b-dc49d71508f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5349ebe8-908e-5984-b145-7ce17efc9294" :question/math-academy-id "q-140912" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find the value of $\\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{1}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+(\\frac{1}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}=\\frac{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}})}{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}})}=2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1a56357-1763-5ddb-a4c1-77a02817dfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6e}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378824} {:answer/id #uuid "20bb06b1-732d-53e4-8a43-31a50273c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi \\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378825} {:answer/id #uuid "91f4af0f-10d5-565f-9a07-659babc43aa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378826} {:answer/id #uuid "e98a25b2-0b21-5c9f-811d-c26eab92dd55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378827} {:answer/id #uuid "2a8d3cc6-3a5f-5a7c-ad92-d3df03abeddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1a56357-1763-5ddb-a4c1-77a02817dfde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6e}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378824} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63e530d-36f3-528b-89d6-88b52987714c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4986375-d8de-524c-9cca-ad92c58d9cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158595" :question/problem "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined as \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate this function at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ we substitute $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},1) & =\\frac{{e}^{y}}{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x}\\,{|}_{(1/2,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6e}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44db07ec-2191-5d66-816a-ba7bc6e87607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379337} {:answer/id #uuid "70e9e99a-aa36-5b43-acc5-2856b465bc6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379338} {:answer/id #uuid "6b44e8c6-a9c5-5929-9a59-23732907aed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379339} {:answer/id #uuid "966effd4-44b8-544e-806d-5177e26f2538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379340} {:answer/id #uuid "61ee2821-c169-5d9c-8600-b826ca6f7e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b44e8c6-a9c5-5929-9a59-23732907aed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379339} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a14916-58ff-5162-b12f-6b1417129ae2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdcec5e6-2531-5f95-ae29-3c0f54fe0692" :question/math-academy-id "q-19135" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=8$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58363d5c-920b-5c26-a09f-feadb48d18af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58363d5c-920b-5c26-a09f-feadb48d18af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379362} :answer-field/id #uuid "569ff198-8871-5c1e-bb50-e29a59fe2e29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59d8a9a7-8383-53f6-9c1f-7b708ece3184" :question/math-academy-id "q-318969" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,m\\,|=12$ and the angle between vectors $m$ and $n$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $m$ onto $n.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m & =|\\,m\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073e29c7-0791-52b8-85a1-d99563246a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "073e29c7-0791-52b8-85a1-d99563246a47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379427} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f9782ff-09d0-5e25-85d1-e4414ec19796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379426} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8895e67a-6d9c-530a-91eb-b89e1679194c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8895e67a-6d9c-530a-91eb-b89e1679194c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379429} :answer-field/id #uuid "f56662ca-9e78-5d95-9eaa-9d4676726b53" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba386268-3918-5f5f-b2cc-ec302545ce83" :question/math-academy-id "q-333273" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=x(y-z),\\, && y(0)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=y(z-x),\\, && z(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=-1$ and $z(0)=-1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-z)=0(-1-(-1))=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y(z-x)=-1(-1-0)=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+0=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |  |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(y-z)=\\frac{1}{2}(-1-(-\\frac{1}{2}))=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =y(z-x)=-1(-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-\\frac{1}{8})=-\\frac{9}{8} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $-\\frac{9}{8}$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈-\\frac{9}{8}$\nand\n$z(1)≈0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4308ef05-e8cd-52d8-8b61-818e73f777ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4308ef05-e8cd-52d8-8b61-818e73f777ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379437} :answer-field/id #uuid "1656d3a8-00dc-5980-b9eb-4de66cbd6595" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379436} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d42ee252-4524-5cd4-abfd-f2de8d7816f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d42ee252-4524-5cd4-abfd-f2de8d7816f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379439} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec116fc-b5e0-5cac-80d1-46b6ea73cd7f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379438} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "966e8422-2b3b-52d7-b8a3-ee67732c2376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "966e8422-2b3b-52d7-b8a3-ee67732c2376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379441} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45190b0-8d89-5e3c-94bb-b5376d3c9849" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04eea417-958a-528d-a120-8313530073b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04eea417-958a-528d-a120-8313530073b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379443} :answer-field/id #uuid "8afb3ecf-2bf8-588e-8368-6b8101186958" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63e300a5-f363-5862-986a-793c350f4f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-333809" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=2x+y+3z,\\, && y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} &=x-y+2z,\\, && z(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=f(x,y,z),\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=g(x,y,z),\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Δy &={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz &={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=-1,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=-1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x+y+3z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0+1+3\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0+1-3 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2z \\\\  & =0-1+2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-1-2 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ |  | $-1$ | $-3$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1+(-\\frac{3}{4})=-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{7}{4}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n$z\\,(\\frac{1}{4})≈-\\frac{7}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78303239-9dfa-50d4-9f1e-ff3706d288ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10+\\sqrt{99}}{4+\\sqrt{15}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78303239-9dfa-50d4-9f1e-ff3706d288ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10+\\sqrt{99}}{4+\\sqrt{15}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379724} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e37d524-66cb-51ab-9745-41c1884781f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddc73c8e-5882-5ea3-8d97-cfcb89e490de" :question/math-academy-id "q-350235" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2x)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{4}^{10}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{10}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡u\\,{|}_{4}^{10} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+\\sqrt{{(10)}^{2}-1})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\sqrt{{(4)}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10+\\sqrt{99})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4+\\sqrt{15}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10+\\sqrt{99}}{4+\\sqrt{15}})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9954780c-4c88-5fe7-8338-f9f18d7d7f65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379850} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a643f3-1047-5955-8824-dee224a33f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379851} {:answer/id #uuid "f20bb04c-a773-5ca0-877d-b6a729829aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379852} {:answer/id #uuid "476f1752-4862-5e0b-9f14-25ed4afb8e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379853} {:answer/id #uuid "7b94fb05-bcb0-53e5-8640-ca0b7f831d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "476f1752-4862-5e0b-9f14-25ed4afb8e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379853} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ffcee2-bec0-577c-a610-f9493369cb2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d53d0a4e-a01f-57ae-9126-10a873bc7b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-49345" :question/problem "![](images/db/db81a26f1761db677e083d724f3ccb0a8963b9de528c15048bf66cea4aca6179.png)\n\nA region is bounded by the curve $y=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)$ and the $x$-axis between $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown above. Find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x){|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is $\\sqrt{2}-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1c473f5-dd16-51e8-972f-8503bdea49f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379933} {:answer/id #uuid "2779943f-aae7-50af-b0c6-2a0c3b6a854a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379934} {:answer/id #uuid "b957948e-1fb9-5dc4-95a5-6c0f15c55de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379935} {:answer/id #uuid "8d86eb8a-95c5-5e77-ae3c-4d5771dc8a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379936} {:answer/id #uuid "91314d14-31ca-547e-a15e-ba542c305c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d86eb8a-95c5-5e77-ae3c-4d5771dc8a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379936} :answer-field/id #uuid "adbe73b9-93eb-5eb7-bc8e-c67c1d6a7d03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6de53f7c-a016-5a05-a942-7eae5f3fae21" :question/math-academy-id "q-71528" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=10$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ae36112-5d67-541f-8618-63c91ad25131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379940} {:answer/id #uuid "e10ff8bd-44a5-5190-acb6-446d018c9704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379941} {:answer/id #uuid "4323e039-1d0d-5d26-a6ff-3ece5246be8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379942} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3e86b8-2db7-52af-8116-994cefeff282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379943} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbbcd7c-a0a4-54df-8f2f-41d5c56772be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa3e86b8-2db7-52af-8116-994cefeff282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379943} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d208d89-fa96-5758-89de-b77ebf18b4c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90337284-e168-5a3e-b426-ab4b7cb72992" :question/math-academy-id "q-71533" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=2$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380304 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c152196f-82a0-580d-9e76-911c9cda9245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380306} {:answer/id #uuid "3f378eb0-558c-5b6c-b837-5d8be473cace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380307} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fdc2d5-8755-529f-9db8-d49d16d9a669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380308} {:answer/id #uuid "cb84ee77-16c0-57ae-9d08-45ac89aff915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{x}{25})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380309} {:answer/id #uuid "1e093665-dbc9-5dd8-a7ac-cdd2be9d66aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e093665-dbc9-5dd8-a7ac-cdd2be9d66aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380310} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b572030-fda2-5b3e-a839-be785ce435a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "905b05fd-d6b9-536d-90cd-bbbe7d0f7435" :question/math-academy-id "q-95857" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{10}{1-25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "This integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $25{x}^{2}={(5x)}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1-25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{artanh}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|5x|<1.$"}]
